window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][75] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> critique · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C11 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your team wants to learn from a competitor's product to inform the roadmap. How do you critique it without copying it, and how do you avoid claiming things you can't know?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;List their features and build the ones we lack.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Critiques the competitor against its purpose and users, and separates what to learn from what to copy.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses labeling discipline (observation, inference, opinion; Verified, Reconstructed), asks why they made each choice, tests whether it fits your users, and respects legal and ethical limits on how information was obtained.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start by being clear on what the teardown is for: to understand what job the competitor does well, where it falls short, and what that implies for our users, not to build a feature list. <em>Method.</em> Apply the critique spine to the competitor: purpose and users (who they serve), flow (walk their main journey as a customer), strengths (what we should respect), ranked issues (where they fall short for a segment we might serve better). Add the strategic lens: how they make money, how they reach users, what protects them. <em>Learn versus copy.</em> For each strength, ask why it works there: their users, their brand, their data, their constraints. A feature that works for them may not fit our segment or capability (the outsider's blind spot, and the steelman). Copy only what solves a problem that we have confirmed our users have; otherwise we import their solution to a problem we do not have. <em>Labeling.</em> I'd write the findings in three columns: <em>observation</em> (what we saw using it, with screenshots), <em>inference</em> (what we think it means, such as &quot;they probably prioritize speed over detail&quot;), and <em>opinion</em> (what we prefer). For anything about their business (pricing, subscriber counts, strategy) I'd use public sources and label each as Verified (a filing or announcement), Reconstructed (inferred from public data, with the inference stated) or unknown. I'd not state their internal metrics as fact. <em>Limits.</em> Use only public information and our own use of the product as a customer. Do not use confidential documents, and do not misrepresent who we are to obtain information; check with legal if unsure. <em>Output.</em> A short memo with ranked opportunities framed as problems for our users, each with a hypothesis and a cheap test (C14). What would change my plan: if testing shows our users do not have the problem the competitor solves, we do not build it, however well it works for them.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Average session length rose 10%, but the median fell. What might be going on?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The data must be wrong,&quot; or &quot;users are engaging more.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains that a mean can rise while the median falls when the distribution is skewed, and says what to check.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives a small numeric example, names several causes (a few very long sessions, a logging or definition change, a shift in the user mix), says which summary answers which decision, and proposes the first check.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Both can be true, and the gap between them is the clue. The mean is pulled by the tail, and the median describes the typical session, so the typical session got shorter while a few long ones got longer. A small example makes it concrete. Ten sessions before: nine of 3 minutes and one of 13. The mean is (27 + 13) ÷ 10 = 4.0 minutes and the median is 3. After: nine of 2.5 minutes and one of 21.5. The mean is (22.5 + 21.5) ÷ 10 = 4.4 minutes, up 10%, and the median is 2.5, down. The typical person spent less time, and one heavy session grew. <em>What could cause it.</em> A few sessions got very long: a tab left open, a bot, a video that plays unattended, or a heavy user using a new feature. The definition of a session changed (for example, a longer idle timeout merges visits into one long session). The mix of users shifted: more casual users with short sessions and the same few power users. Or a real change: a new feature helps some people go deeper and makes others leave sooner. <em>What I'd check first.</em> The distribution itself: a histogram and the 25th, 50th, 75th and 95th percentiles before and after. Then whether the session definition or logging changed, whether bots or idle sessions are included, and whether the mix of new and returning users changed. I'd break the number down by segment (C12.7) to see if the pattern is a mix effect. <em>Which number to use.</em> It depends on the decision. If the question is &quot;is the typical user better off?&quot;, the median and the share of sessions above a useful length. If it is &quot;how many total hours are we delivering?&quot;, the mean, since totals are sums. I'd report both with the group sizes, and I would not call a 10% rise in the average an improvement until I know which of these it is.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Explain a 95% confidence interval to a non-technical executive.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's the range where the true value is 95% likely to be,&quot; or a technical definition the executive cannot use.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives a plain statement with an example, explains what it says about uncertainty, and links it to a decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Avoids the common misreading, uses counts, ties the interval to a decision threshold, and says how more data would narrow it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd say it in terms of the decision. &quot;We measured how many customers buy, in a sample of 10,000: 4.0% bought. We don't know the true rate for all customers, because we only saw some. The confidence interval is the range of true rates that are consistent with what we saw. Here it runs from about 3.6% to 4.4%. So when someone says 'conversion is 4%,' what the data really says is 'somewhere around 3.6% to 4.4%'.&quot; <em>Why the width is what it is.</em> &quot;The more customers we measure, the narrower the range. To make the range half as wide, we need about four times as many customers. So a bigger sample buys precision, and it buys it slowly.&quot; <em>How to use it.</em> &quot;If we need conversion to be at least 4.5% to justify the project, then the whole range is below the bar, so we should not expect to clear it. If the bar were 3.5%, the whole range is above it, and we can be fairly confident. If the bar were 4.0%, the range straddles it, and we don't know yet.&quot; <em>What it does not mean.</em> &quot;It is not the range that 95% of customers fall in. Individual customers either buy or not. And I'd be careful with the phrase '95% chance the true value is inside': the careful statement is that if we repeated the whole measurement many times, about 95% of the ranges we'd produce would contain the true value. For a decision, the plain version above is close enough.&quot; I'd close with the one thing the executive should take away: ask for the range, not only the number.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An A/B test shows conversion up 2% (relative) with p = 0.04. Do you ship?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, it's significant,&quot; or &quot;no, 2% is too small,&quot; without reasoning.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for the absolute change, the sample size and the interval, and compares with the cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sets the decision threshold from the cost, reads the interval against it, checks the test's validity (sample ratio, peeking, multiple metrics), and gives a decision with the condition that would change it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd not answer from the p-value alone. I'd ask five things. <em>The numbers.</em> Suppose the base rate is 5.00% and the variant is 5.10%, which is a 2% relative and 0.1 point absolute lift. With 400,000 users in each arm, p comes out near 0.04. The 95% interval for the lift is about +0.004 to +0.196 points, or from nothing to about four percent in relative terms. <em>What it is worth.</em> Suppose 10,000,000 visitors a month and $10 of contribution per extra purchase (invented). The best estimate, +0.1 point, is 10,000 purchases and about $100,000 a month. The interval runs from about $4,000 to about $196,000 a month. <em>The threshold.</em> Suppose the change costs $50,000 to build and $5,000 a month to maintain (invented). The upkeep is covered if the lift is at least 0.005 points, which is 500 purchases a month. The lower end of the interval, 0.004 points, is just under that, and the best estimate is twenty times it. So the interval mostly clears the cost and the bottom edge touches it. <em>Validity.</em> I'd check that the arms are the planned size (a mismatch can signal an assignment problem, covered in C14), that nobody stopped the test the first time it crossed the threshold, and that the 2% was the one metric chosen in advance, not the best of several. If we looked at five metrics and report the best, a p of 0.04 is much weaker. <em>The decision.</em> &quot;I would ship it, with a guardrail on a metric the change could harm, because the whole interval is above zero, most of it is above the cost, and the change is cheap and reversible. I'd tell the team the gain is probably real and could be small, and that I wouldn't plan the roadmap around +$100,000 a month. I'd change my mind if the check showed the test was not run as planned, or if the maintenance cost were several times higher, in which case the interval would straddle break-even and I'd extend the test.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Roughly how many users do you need to detect a one-point lift on a 10% conversion rate, and how long would that take?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A few thousand,&quot; or &quot;as many as possible.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses a rule or formula, states the assumptions (power, significance), and converts to duration.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows what happens at half the lift, states that the rule is approximate, uses whole weeks, and gives options if the test is too long.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll use the rule of 16, which I'd say is a sketch: for 80% power at the usual 5% significance, users per arm ≈ 16 × p(1 − p) ÷ δ². Here p = 0.10, so p(1 − p) = 0.09, and δ = 0.01. That is 16 × 0.09 ÷ 0.0001 = 14,400 per arm, so about 28,800 in total. An exact calculation gives about 14,750 per arm, so the rule is within a few percent. <em>Duration.</em> Assume 4,000 eligible users a day, split evenly: 2,000 per arm per day. 14,400 ÷ 2,000 = 7.2 days. I'd run two full weeks, not eight days, so that each weekday appears in both arms equally and any weekly cycle averages out. <em>If the lift were half as large.</em> For δ = 0.5 points the requirement is 16 × 0.09 ÷ 0.005² = 57,600 per arm: four times as many for half the lift. That is 28.8 days at the same traffic, so five whole weeks. This is why the smallest effect worth detecting should come from the decision, not the calendar. <em>If the test is too long.</em> I'd raise the minimum effect if the decision allows it, narrow the population to people the change targets, measure a metric closer to the change so there is less noise, or accept lower power and release in stages with a guardrail. I'd not set the effect size to fit the traffic and then report whatever comes out. <em>Caveats.</em> The rule assumes independent users, a two-arm test on a proportion, and that I have chosen the effect and the duration before starting. If users interact (a social feature) or if I will look at the data repeatedly, a data scientist should design it.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An account-takeover detector catches 95% of takeovers and wrongly flags 3% of legitimate logins. Takeovers are 0.2% of logins. What should happen when the detector flags a login?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Lock the account, since the detector is 95% accurate.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes the share of flags that are real with counts, and sees that most flags are false alarms.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Matches the response to the cost of each error (a challenge is cheap, a lock is not), shows how the number moves with the false-alarm rate and the base rate, and sets metrics for both kinds of error.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd build the counts for 100,000 logins. - Takeovers: 0.2% of 100,000 is 200. The detector flags 95% of them: 190. - Legitimate logins: 99,800. The detector flags 3%: 2,994. - Total flags: 190 + 2,994 = 3,184. Of these, 190 are real. The chance that a flagged login is a takeover is 190 ÷ 3,184, about 6%. Roughly 94 flags in 100 are false alarms. &quot;95% accurate&quot; is the catch rate among takeovers, a different number from the share of flags that are right. <em>What follows for the response.</em> Locking the account on a flag would lock 2,994 legitimate users for every 190 attackers, which is harmful and expensive. A cheap challenge (a code sent to the user's phone) costs the legitimate user a few seconds and stops most attackers. So the response should match the cost of a wrong call: a challenge for a flag, and a lock only when the evidence is much stronger (more signals, or a failed challenge). <em>How the number moves.</em> If the false-alarm rate fell from 3% to 0.5%, legitimate flags would be about 499 and the share of flags that are real would rise to 190 ÷ 689, about 28%. If the base rate fell (attacks drop) with the same detector, the share of real flags falls too, so the precision must be recomputed over time and not assumed fixed. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of takeovers stopped. Guardrails: the share of legitimate logins challenged (here about 3%), challenge failure rate among legitimate users, and support contacts about lockouts. Counter metric: the time attackers spend inside an account before detection, which a looser threshold would shorten. I'd set the threshold with the security and support teams, and I'd say which cost is higher in this product, since the right balance differs by what the account holds.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Average order value fell from $24.00 to $21.82 after a marketing push, but it rose in every country. What happened and what do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's an error,&quot; or &quot;the marketing push hurt order value.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies a mix shift (Simpson's paradox) and computes a like-for-like comparison.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notices that the shift itself (fewer orders from the high-value country) is the real question, reports a mix-adjusted figure and the revenue effect, and proposes where to look.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> This is Simpson's paradox: a rate that rises within every group and falls overall because the mix of groups changed. I'd confirm with the numbers (invented). | | Before: orders | Before: average value | After: orders | After: average value | |---|---|---|---|---| | Country X | 1,000 | $60 | 200 | $62 | | Country Y | 9,000 | $20 | 9,800 | $21 | | All | 10,000 | $24.00 | 10,000 | $21.82 | Before, revenue was 1,000 × $60 + 9,000 × $20 = $240,000, an average of $24.00. After, it was 200 × $62 + 9,800 × $21 = $12,400 + $205,800 = $218,200, an average of $21.82. Order value rose in X ($60 to $62) and in Y ($20 to $21). It fell overall because X, the high-value country, went from 10% of orders to 2%. <em>A like-for-like figure.</em> At the old mix (10% X, 90% Y) and the new averages: 0.1 × $62 + 0.9 × $21 = $6.20 + $18.90 = $25.10, against $24.00 before, up about 4.6%. Within each country things improved. <em>The real question.</em> The headline average is not the main problem. What matters is that orders from X fell from 1,000 to 200, and total revenue fell from $240,000 to $218,200 (about 9%) on the same 10,000 orders. The push brought more orders in Y and X shrank. I'd ask why: did the campaign pull spend and attention away from X, was there an outage, a regulatory problem, a competitor or a shipping change in X, or did the push change who is counted in X? I'd look at X's funnel before and after, by week, to find when it dropped. <em>What I'd report.</em> Order value by country and a mix-adjusted figure, with order counts, and revenue in total. I'd not tell leadership that the push &quot;lowered order value,&quot; since within each country it rose, and I'd not say it succeeded, since total revenue fell. The decision depends on the unit economics in each country (margin per order, cost to acquire) and on whether the shift toward Y is intended. I'd change my view if X's drop were found to be unrelated to the push, in which case two separate stories sit in one number.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c12-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C12-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C12 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your data scientist says the test is underpowered and recommends running six more weeks. The launch is in two weeks. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Launch anyway,&quot; or &quot;wait six weeks,&quot; without weighing the stakes.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Describes what the current data does and does not say, weighs the cost of delay against the cost of being wrong, and offers options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies both sides, distinguishes reversible from irreversible decisions, proposes a staged rollout with a stopping rule and a long-run holdout, and keeps the data scientist as a partner.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a decision under uncertainty, not as a fight between the test and the date. First I'd ask the data scientist for what the data says now: the estimate and its interval, and what lift the test was designed to detect. Suppose the lift is +0.3 points with an interval of −0.5 to +1.1, and the team's threshold for worthwhile is +0.5. <em>What the data says.</em> &quot;Underpowered&quot; means the interval is wide: it includes harm, nothing, and a gain above the threshold. I can't say the change works, and I can't say it doesn't. <em>The two costs.</em> Delay: if the true lift is +0.5 points on 6,000,000 monthly visitors at $10 per purchase (invented), the change is worth about 30,000 purchases, $300,000 a month, so six weeks' delay costs roughly $420,000 of benefit if the change is good. Being wrong: if the true effect is −0.5 points the change costs about $300,000 a month, and the question is how fast we would see it and undo it. <em>Reversibility is the deciding fact (C02).</em> If the change can be undone in a day, the cost of being wrong is limited to the days it runs, and the cost of delay is real. If it cannot be undone (a migration, a public commitment), I'd wait or reduce the risk differently. <em>Options.</em> (1) Launch in stages: 10% of users, then 50%, then all, with a guardrail and a written stopping rule (stop if the guardrail metric drops by more than a set amount), while the test continues in the background with a holdout group that never gets the change, so the full six weeks of data arrive anyway. (2) Narrow the metric or population so the test is more sensitive, for example the first-step completion rate among new users, and check whether the shorter test is now adequate. (3) Raise the minimum effect: if the change only pays above +0.5 points and the data cannot rule out a smaller effect, say that the question is whether the team would act on a result of +0.3. (4) Delay six weeks, if the decision is hard to reverse. <em>My recommendation.</em> If reversible: option 1 with the holdout. It keeps the date, caps the downside and still produces the evidence. I'd say to leadership: &quot;We don't yet know if this helps. We'll release in steps with a stop rule, and we'll know by [date]. If it hurts, we'll pull it within a day.&quot; I'd thank the data scientist for raising the issue and ask them to own the stopping rule. I'd change my mind if the guardrail could not be measured quickly, in which case staging gives no protection and delay is safer.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Weekly sign-ups fell 3% this week. Is it a problem?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, any drop is a problem, so I'd investigate,&quot; or &quot;no, 3% is small.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares the change with the normal variation and a baseline before deciding, and says what would trigger a deeper look.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes a noise band from history, notes the limits of a short history and of watching many metrics, proposes a cheap first check, and sets a monitoring rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd answer &quot;is it a problem?&quot; with &quot;compared with what?&quot; Three questions first: how much do weekly sign-ups normally move, is this week's data complete, and is it an unusual week (a holiday)? Suppose the last eight weeks were 10,100, 9,900, 10,200, 10,000, 9,800, 10,100, 10,050 and 9,950. The mean is about 10,010 and the standard deviation about 130, which is about 1.3% of the mean. This week's 9,700 is 3.1% below the mean and about 2.5 standard deviations below it. That is unusual but not overwhelming: with only eight weeks of history my estimate of the normal swing is itself uncertain, and a team that watches dozens of metrics every week will see a 2.5-sigma move by chance from time to time (C12). So I would not open a full investigation, and I would not ignore it. A cheap first check, ten minutes: is the data complete, was it a holiday week, and do the segments (platform, channel, country) show one place where it fell? If one segment explains most of the drop, I'd look deeper there. If the drop is spread evenly and there is no cause, I'd set a rule and watch: if next week is also below 9,850 (about the mean minus one and a quarter standard deviations), or if the drop persists for two weeks, I'd investigate fully. The decision I'd state: &quot;It is slightly outside the normal range; I'd check segments and the calendar now, and escalate if it persists.&quot; What would change my mind: a concentrated segment drop, or a related metric (activation, revenue) moving too.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Average watch time per user on a large video platform fell 3% over two weeks. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Content must have gotten worse,&quot; or &quot;run an experiment.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Confirms the change, segments by user type, and checks for a mix shift.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Splits rate and mix with numbers, notes that a growing share of new users lowers an average without any change in behavior, and says what to track instead.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information and use. First step zero: the metric is average minutes per user, the window is two weeks, the data is complete and a second metric, sessions, agrees. A 3% move over two weeks is a slide, not a step, which points away from a release or an outage and toward a change in who is using the product or how. <em>Segment by tenure.</em> An average over users is a mix of new and established viewers. Suppose established users watch 90 minutes in the period and new users 40, and new users were 10% of the base and are now 15% (a marketing push or a launch in a new country). - Before: 0.90 × 90 + 0.10 × 40 = 81 + 4 = 85 minutes. - After: 0.85 × 90 + 0.15 × 40 = 76.5 + 6 = 82.5 minutes, which is −2.9%. If established users still watch 90 and new users still watch 40, the rate effect is zero and the mix effect is (0.85 − 0.90) × 90 + (0.15 − 0.10) × 40 = −4.5 + 2.0 = −2.5, which is the whole drop. A fall in the average with no change in anyone's behavior. <em>Confirm.</em> I'd check that established users' watch time is flat, and that the new users' watch time is in line with earlier cohorts at the same age (C05's cohort reading). I'd also check the other mix candidates: device (more phone, less TV), country, and content type. <em>Decision.</em> If the mix explains it, nothing is broken: more new users is growth, and new users' watch time grows with tenure. I'd change how the metric is reported (by cohort and tenure), and I'd say to stakeholders: &quot;The average fell because new users are a larger share; established users are unchanged.&quot; I'd keep a watch on new-user watch time by age as an input to activation (C05). What would change my mind: if established users' watch time fell, the drop would be a real change in behavior and I'd look at content, recommendations and releases. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that analytical prompts at Google concern its own products at scale <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Daily app downloads on an app store fell 8% week over week (invented). Diagnose.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Maybe fewer people want apps, or there is a competitor.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Confirms the change, segments by region, device and release, and checks the calendar and store features.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies contributions, nests cuts to a specific country, builds a ledger with predictions, apportions the change and decides with owners named.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information and use. Step zero: downloads per day, averaged over the week, from 1,000,000 to 920,000. I'd check that the week is complete, the definition (does it count re-downloads and updates?) is unchanged, and the normal week-to-week swing, which I'd expect to be a couple of percent. A second source, such as developer-reported installs, should agree. <em>Locate by region (FW-C13-04).</em> Suppose: North America 300,000 to 295,000; Europe 250,000 to 247,000; Asia-Pacific 350,000 to 278,000; other 100,000 to 100,000. That is −5,000, −3,000, −72,000 and 0, a total of −80,000. Asia-Pacific explains 72,000 of 80,000, 90%. <em>Nest.</em> Within Asia-Pacific, one country goes from 150,000 to 85,000, down 65,000: 81% of the whole drop (65 of 80), while the other countries in the region fell 7,000. <em>Ledger (FW-C13-06).</em> | Hypothesis | If true, we would also see | Cheapest check | Status | |---|---|---|---| | Data or pipeline lag in that country | Other metrics from the country (sessions, purchases) look normal; the fall disappears when data settles | Second source and later data | Check first | | A feature placement or promotion in that store ended | A fall concentrated in a few titles that had been featured, starting on a known date | The editorial calendar; downloads by title | Likely | | A holiday or seasonal pattern | The same fall in that country in the same week last year; across all apps | Last year's week; downloads of other categories | Check | | A release or policy change in the country | A fall at a specific time, perhaps after a store or system change | Release and policy log | Check | | A network or payment disruption | Failures in related flows, status reports, a fall in all downloads | Status pages and error logs | Check | | Bot or incentivized downloads removed | A fall concentrated in a few apps with unusual earlier spikes | Fraud team's actions | Check | <em>Timing, location and size.</em> A candidate cause has to explain a 65,000 fall in one country starting on a date. A ended feature placement, a holiday or a disruption could each do it; the checks separate them in an hour. <em>Decision.</em> If it is a calendar or featured-slot effect, no action beyond an annotation and a watch for the rebound. If it is a disruption, escalate to the owner and communicate to developers. If it is data, fix the pipeline and restate. I'd tell the store's business owner and the regional lead what we know, the size, and the next update. Prevention: alert on downloads by country, not only the global total. What would change my mind: if the fall were spread across all regions, I'd look at measurement or a global change instead. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that analytical prompts at Apple include diagnosing movements in services metrics <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Apple data or system behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> After a marketing campaign, sign-ups rose 25%, but 30-day retention of new members fell from 70% to 60%. Is the campaign working?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Sign-ups rose, so yes,&quot; or &quot;retention fell, so no.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates the campaign cohort from the others, computes the retention of the incremental members, and compares with cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows the arithmetic, treats the drop as a mix shift and not a change in the product, looks at a longer horizon, and sets a decision rule on cost per retained member.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> This is a mix question. The headline retention fell, but I don't yet know whether the product got worse or the campaign brought in a different kind of member. I know the product only from public information. <em>The arithmetic.</em> Before the campaign: 100,000 sign-ups at 70% retained, so 70,000 retained at 30 days. After: 125,000 sign-ups at 60% retained, so 75,000. Suppose the 100,000 baseline sign-ups still retain at 70%. Then the 25,000 incremental sign-ups account for 75,000 − 70,000 = 5,000 retained, which is a retention of 5,000 ÷ 25,000 = 20%. The overall rate fell from 70% to 60% only because a large group retaining at 20% joined a group retaining at 70%. The rate effect on the baseline group is zero; it is a mix effect. <em>What this says about the campaign.</em> It added 5,000 retained members, a 7% increase over 70,000, and its members are far less likely to stay. Whether that is a success depends on the cost. If the campaign cost $750,000 (invented), it cost $30 per sign-up and $150 per member retained at 30 days (750,000 ÷ 5,000). I would ask what a retained member is worth over their life. If it is above $150 in margin, the campaign pays; if it is below, it does not. <em>What else I'd check.</em> The campaign cohort by channel, country and plan, since part may be good and part poor. A longer horizon: some members come late, and 30-day retention may understate it, so I'd look at 90-day retention for the earlier cohorts. Abuse: free-trial sign-ups that never intended to stay. And the baseline group's retention, to confirm it did not change. <em>Decision.</em> Don't judge by retention alone. Judge by cost per retained member against value, and optimize targeting: shift spend to the channels where the incremental members retain better. I'd report retention for the baseline and campaign cohorts separately, so the average does not hide either. What would change my mind: if the baseline group's retention had also dropped, the problem would be the product or its early experience, not the mix. <em>Company note.</em> Netflix is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of data-informed prompts on sign-ups and retention <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Netflix data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Battery-life complaints rose after a system software update. Diagnose and decide.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Roll back the update,&quot; or &quot;it's probably perception.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates complaints from measured drain, controls for who updates early, checks segments such as device age, and weighs reversibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes transient from persistent effects, targets the response by device group, states a decision rule with thresholds, and sets out the communication.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the situation only in general terms and nothing here describes any real product. The key facts first, with assumptions: the update was released four days ago to 40% of eligible devices through a staged rollout; complaints are support contacts and feedback that mention battery. <em>Is it real?</em> Complaints are a self-selected and lagging signal. People pay more attention to battery right after an update, and people who update early differ from those who wait. I'd compare complaints per 10,000 devices: updated 12, not updated 4 (invented). For the previous release's first four days, updated devices had 8 and not-updated 4, so there is a baseline gap of 4 from early adoption and attention. This release's excess over that is (12 − 4) − (8 − 4) = 4 complaints per 10,000 devices. It is a real but smaller signal than the raw ratio of 3 to 1. <em>Measure drain, not only complaints.</em> From device telemetry (with the privacy limits in force), compare battery use in standard conditions between updated devices and a matched control with the same model, age and usage. Suppose the first 48 hours show drain +9% against control and by day four +2% (invented). A fast decay suggests a transient effect, for example background work after an update, which is a hypothesis to test and not a claim about any specific cause. <em>Segment.</em> By device model and battery health: suppose newer devices show +1% at day four and older devices with worn batteries +6% (invented). So the effect persists for one group. <em>Taxonomy.</em> Data: telemetry coverage and complaint counting. Product change: the update itself (and any app update shipped that week). External: a popular third-party app misbehaving after the change. Mix: older devices updating later. Abuse: not relevant. The evidence points at a transient effect for most and a persistent one for older devices. <em>Decide (FW-C13-07).</em> Severity is moderate unless there are heat or safety signals, which would mean pause immediately. Reversibility is low: an installed update cannot easily be undone, and 60% of devices have not yet received it. So the lever is the rollout. My decision rule: if any device group shows persistent excess drain above +5% at day seven, or any thermal or safety signal appears, pause the rollout for that group. By that rule: pause the rollout for older devices, continue for newer ones, and ship a point release that addresses the persistent drain. Meanwhile set expectations for everyone updating: &quot;battery may drain faster for a day or two after updating.&quot; <em>Communicate.</em> Five lines for support and leadership: what (battery complaints up, excess 4 per 10,000 devices over baseline), since when (the update's release), what we know (transient for most, persistent on older devices), what we're doing (paused for older devices, point release in progress, owner named), next update in 24 hours. Prevention: add a device-age cut and a drain guardrail to the staged rollout so a pause is triggered automatically. What would change my mind: if telemetry showed drain at day four equal to control in all groups, I'd continue everywhere and treat the complaints as attention. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that diagnosis prompts at Apple involve behavior after updates and the limits on reversing them <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real update, telemetry or process.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> After a new &quot;share to story&quot; button launched, daily feed posts per user fell 6%. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The button is hurting feed posting, so remove it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks where the posting went, compares total creation, and redefines the metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the net, considers the effect on feed supply for viewers, notes the incentive created by owning a metric for one surface, and sets a guardrail.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information. Step zero: the metric counts posts to one surface, the feed. A fall in a surface metric after launching a feature on another surface is a signal to ask where the missing activity went (C13.6). Suppose feed posts per user fell from 1.00 to 0.94 a day, while story shares per user rose from 0.10 to 0.17. <em>Total creation.</em> Before: 1.00 + 0.10 = 1.10 pieces of content per user. After: 0.94 + 0.17 = 1.11. Net: about +1%. People did not stop sharing; some of it moved from the feed to stories. The metric's blind spot made a flat total look like a 6% decline. <em>What it does not settle.</em> Whether the move is good. Questions: did the same people shift (cannibalization), or did one group post less to the feed while another started posting to stories (users who both create and view)? Does the viewer's side change: fewer feed posts means less for viewers to see, so I'd check feed consumption and time spent, and whether stories offset it. Is story content as valuable to creators and viewers as feed content (a story disappears, a feed post persists)? And does any group, such as creators who rely on feed reach, lose? <em>The incentive problem.</em> If a team is measured on feed posts per user, it may resist features that move posting elsewhere even when the company gains. This is <a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a> (C05) in organizational form. The fix is to measure the outcome, total content creation and the health of both supply and consumption, and not the surface. <em>Decision.</em> Don't remove the button. Redefine the creation metric as total creation across surfaces, keep feed posts per user as a diagnostic, and add guardrails: feed consumption per viewer and the share of creators whose total creation falls. Tell the owners of both surfaces what the data says. What would change my mind: if total creation had fallen, or if feed viewers' time spent fell without a matching rise in stories, the move would be a loss, and I'd revisit the design (for example, how the button is placed). <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prompts at Meta can involve how a metric hides a shift between surfaces and how to weigh it <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Overnight, daily sign-ups jumped 40%. How do you respond?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Great news; let's find out what we did right and do more of it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats the rise with suspicion, checks the data and the source, and looks at what the new sign-ups do afterward.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses post-arrival behavior to test whether they are real people, computes the implied activation, acts on the source, and protects metrics and costs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as a diagnosis, with more suspicion than for a drop, because good news is accepted too easily (FW-C13-08). Step zero: a jump of this size and speed with no launch or campaign planned is unusual. Check that the data is complete and not double counted, and that the definition did not change. <em>Locate.</em> Normal is 10,000 a day, today is 14,000, so 4,000 extra. By channel, country and device: suppose nearly all of the extra comes from one country and one referral source, with a narrow range of email domains and device types. A concentration like that is a sign of non-human traffic. <em>Test with behavior after arrival.</em> Real people activate and return. Suppose 45% of normal sign-ups activate within a day, which on 10,000 is 4,500. Today, 4,508 activated. So the 4,000 extra sign-ups produced only 8 activations, 0.2%. The extra sign-ups did nothing, which is what bots or fake accounts do. (Activation is a <a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a> for a real user, C05.) <em>Taxonomy.</em> Abuse (family 5) fits timing, location and size. Data error is ruled out by the second source. A product or external cause would show real activation. <em>Decide.</em> Severity: moderate to high. Fake accounts cost money (verification messages, storage, support), distort every metric and can harm real users if they are used for spam. Reversibility: easy for sign-up controls. Actions: block or rate-limit the source and add friction for the pattern (a challenge for sign-ups from it), remove the fake accounts from the metrics (restate the day with a note on the definition), and tell the integrity team and the owners of the dashboards. I would not report the 40% as growth. <em>Communicate.</em> &quot;Sign-ups rose 40% yesterday. About 4,000 came from one source and did not activate: 8 activations against about 1,800 expected from that many real sign-ups. We believe they are fake. We are blocking the source and restating the metric. Owner: integrity. Next update at 14:00.&quot; <em>Prevent.</em> Monitor sign-ups by source with an activation check, and alert when a source's volume jumps while its activation is near zero. What would change my mind: if the extra sign-ups activated at a normal rate, they are real, and I'd find out what we did (a mention, a campaign) and whether it will last or is a one-day spike that pulls sign-ups forward.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you count daily active users from an events table, and what can go wrong with the obvious query?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Count the rows per day.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Counts distinct users per day and says how &quot;active&quot; is defined.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> States the definition (which events, which time zone), the grain, the distinct count, and the checks (total against a known number, bots and test accounts, definition drift), and gives the query.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Definition first.</em> &quot;Active&quot; must be written down: which events count (any event, or a meaningful one), in which time zone the day is cut, and which accounts are excluded (test accounts, known bots). Without that, two people produce two numbers. <em>Grain.</em> The events table has one row per user per event, so one user can have many rows in a day. The result I want has one row per day with one number: people, not events. <code>sql SELECT event_date, COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS active_users FROM events WHERE event IN ('signup', 'activated') -- whichever events define &quot;active&quot; GROUP BY event_date ORDER BY event_date; </code> <em>What goes wrong.</em> Counting rows (<code>COUNT(*)</code>) overstates users: on the practice data, 4 January has 268 rows for those two events and 241 different users. Other failures: a day boundary in the wrong time zone, which shifts activity between days; test and bot accounts that inflate the count; and a definition that changes between reports. <em>Check.</em> Compare the total with a known number (the practice data has 5,000 users, so no day can have more than 5,000 active users), check the number is plausible against last week's, and read a few rows. If the number will drive a decision, get it a second way, and run the six checks of FW-C21-04 with the two added in C13.6a. <em>Signals.</em> The definition stated first; grain; the distinct count; the failure modes; checks.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Two dashboards show different week-1 retention for the same signup cohort. How do you find out why?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;One of them is wrong; I'd ask the data team.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists the likely causes: definition, population, window and joins.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Writes both definitions in the same four parts, reproduces both numbers with one query side by side, finds the first place they diverge, and ends with one documented definition and an owner.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Treat it as a definition problem until shown otherwise.</em> I'd write each dashboard's metric in four parts (FW-C05-01): the numerator (who counts as retained), the denominator (which users are in the cohort), the window (which days after signup) and the population (filters, such as activated users only or excluding test accounts). Differences show up in these four places, and also in the time zone and in the as-of date. <em>Reproduce both with one query.</em> On the practice data, the same 5,000 users give different answers depending on the window: 745 users returned in days 7 to 13 after signup, and 107 returned in days 1 to 7. <code>sql SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN CAST(julianday(r.event_date) - julianday(u.signup_date) AS INT) BETWEEN 1 AND 7 THEN u.user_id END) AS returned_days_1_to_7, COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN CAST(julianday(r.event_date) - julianday(u.signup_date) AS INT) BETWEEN 7 AND 13 THEN u.user_id END) AS returned_days_7_to_13 FROM users u JOIN events r ON r.user_id = u.user_id AND r.event = 'returned'; </code> Output: 107 and 745. Note that day 7 sits in both windows, so even the boundary needs a rule. <em>Other causes to check.</em> A join that multiplies rows (fan-out, C21.4: 7,574 rows for 5,000 users on the practice data); an inner join that drops users who did nothing; a cohort that has not had its full window yet; a time-zone difference at the day boundary; one dashboard counting from a different table. <em>Close it.</em> Agree one written definition with its owner, say which dashboard now follows it, and note the change date so that trend lines are not compared across the break. <em>Signals.</em> Four-part definitions; one reproducing query; the first point of divergence; documented ownership.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c13-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C13-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C13 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Describe, in SQL or in words, how you would compute week-1 retention by signup week and channel, and say what could mislead you when you read it.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Group users by week and channel and count who came back.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Gives the right grain and joins, and notes small cohorts.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Builds a per-user flag first, groups after, defines the window, handles incomplete cohorts, reads rates with sample sizes and noise, and names the mix and survivorship traps.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Per-user flag first, group after.</em> I'd build one row per user with a retention flag, then aggregate. That fixes the grain and avoids fan-out from the events table. <code>sql WITH base AS ( SELECT u.user_id, strftime('%Y-%W', u.signup_date) AS signup_week, u.channel, MAX(CASE WHEN e.event = 'returned' AND CAST(julianday(e.event_date) - julianday(u.signup_date) AS INT) BETWEEN 7 AND 13 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS returned FROM users u LEFT JOIN events e ON e.user_id = u.user_id GROUP BY u.user_id) SELECT signup_week, channel, COUNT(*) AS signups, SUM(returned) AS returned, ROUND(100.0 * SUM(returned) / COUNT(*), 1) AS return_pct FROM base GROUP BY signup_week, channel ORDER BY signup_week, channel; </code> On the practice data the output is, for signup weeks 2027-01, 2027-02 and 2027-03: organic 18.0%, 14.0% and 16.5% (771, 713 and 764 signups); paid 9.3%, 6.9% and 9.0% (592, 567 and 586); referral 24.2%, 19.7% and 25.6% (335, 340 and 332). <em>What could mislead.</em> (1) <em>Incomplete cohorts:</em> the latest week may not have had the full window yet, so its rate is understated; I'd state the as-of date and drop cohorts that are not complete. (2) <em>Small cells and noise:</em> a channel-week with 335 users has a wide margin (several points), and the middle week here is lower in all three channels even though the data were built with the same return probability each week; I'd check the noise band before explaining a difference (C13.3). (3) <em>Mix:</em> an overall retention number blends channels, so a rise in paid signups lowers it with no change inside any channel (Worked example 7); report by channel. (4) <em>Survivorship:</em> if I restrict to users who activated, I answer a different question; I'd say which population I mean. (5) <em>Definition drift:</em> the window and the event must match the dashboard's definition. (6) <em>Many comparisons:</em> nine cells will show a few differences by chance. <em>Check.</em> The cells' signups should add to 5,000, the rates should lie between 0 and 100, and a second method (a pivot table on a sample) should agree on at least one cell (the checks of FW-C21-04 and C13.6a). <em>Signals.</em> Per-user flag then group; the window; incomplete cohorts; noise; mix; survivorship; checks.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c14-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C14-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C14 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> We launched a redesigned checkout last week and conversion went up. Why not just compare before and after?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;That is fine, the number went up.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names that other things change over time, so the comparison is confounded, and proposes a control.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies a seasonal or concurrent effect, offers a method that works without randomization, and says what evidence level the answer reaches.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A before-and-after comparison answers &quot;what changed?&quot; and not &quot;what did the launch change?&quot; Anything else that moved over the same weeks is mixed into the number: a season, a promotion, a competitor, a traffic mix change, or regression to the mean if we launched because conversion had been low. An example (invented). Conversion was 5.0% the week before launch and 5.4% the week after: a gain of 0.4 points. But a market where we did not launch the redesign went from 5.0% to 5.3% over the same weeks, perhaps because of a seasonal rise. The best estimate of the redesign's effect is the difference of the two changes: 0.4 − 0.3 = +0.1 points, a quarter of the naive figure. That is a difference-in-differences estimate (C14.6), and it relies on the assumption that the two markets would have moved alike without the launch. <em>What I'd do.</em> If the launch can still be randomized, the best option is to keep a holdout: put a random 5% or 10% of users on the old checkout for a few weeks and compare. If it has already shipped everywhere, use a comparison group (a market, a platform or a delayed rollout), check that the two groups moved together before the launch, and say that the evidence is weaker than an experiment because unmeasured differences between the groups could remain. <em>How I'd say it.</em> &quot;I'd not call the 0.4 points the effect. I'd compare with a control, which suggests about 0.1 points, and I'd tell the team it is a rung-5 estimate, not a randomized one.&quot; What would change my mind: if the comparison market's pre-launch trend differed from ours, the estimate would be unreliable and I'd look for a better comparison.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c14-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C14-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C14 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An experiment was planned 50/50. The readout shows 5,100 users in one arm and 4,900 in the other. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's close enough,&quot; or &quot;the test is broken.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes how unusual the gap is and knows the sample-ratio check.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes a borderline from a clear mismatch, sets a strict alarm threshold, and lists causes and actions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Compute how unusual it is. There are 10,000 users, so the expected count per arm is 5,000. The standard deviation of an arm's count is √(10,000 × 0.25) = 50. A count of 5,100 is 100 above, which is 2 standard deviations (p ≈ 0.046). By chance, that happens about once in 22 tests, so on its own it is not alarming. The usual practice is a stricter alarm for this check, for example p below 0.001, which at this sample size would be a gap of about 165 users, or 3.3 standard deviations. <em>So what do I do?</em> Not discard the test. Note it as borderline and look at the pattern: is the gap stable across days, or does it grow? Check the assignment log against the analytics counts, and whether the arms are logged identically. If more data keep the gap at about 2%, the standard deviation shrinks and the mismatch becomes clear, and then I treat the test as invalid. <em>Contrast with a clear case.</em> If 1,000,000 users were assigned and the readout showed 508,000 and 492,000, the gap is 8,000 against a standard deviation of 500: 16 standard deviations. That is not chance. Something differs between arms in who is counted (a bot filter on one arm, a slower variant that loses users before logging, a failed redirect). I would not read any metric from such a test, because the missing users are rarely typical. <em>Decision.</em> For 5,100 against 4,900: continue, check the logs, and decide when the test ends. For the clear mismatch: stop, find the cause, fix and rerun. What would change my view: if the assignment log itself showed 5,100 and 4,900, the randomizer would be at fault, which is rarer and more serious.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c14-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C14-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C14 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design an experiment for a new layout of a search results page.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A/B test it and look at clicks.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Chooses the unit, the metrics (with guardrails), the size and the duration, and decides on a rule.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that clicks can mislead when a layout answers the question directly, picks success and satisfaction measures, handles query-type differences and novelty, and sets guardrails on speed, advertisers and long-term return.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information. The decision: ship the new layout if it helps people find what they want. Hypothesis: the layout makes the answer easier to reach. <em>Unit.</em> The user, not the query, because a person sees the layout across many queries and learns it; randomizing queries would show one person different layouts and blur learning effects. Assignment by a stable hash of a user identifier, with a consistent identifier for signed-out users where possible. <em>Metrics.</em> A trap here is to use clicks as the primary: if the new layout answers the question on the page, clicks fall while satisfaction rises (people get the answer and leave). So the primary should measure success: the share of search sessions in which the person gets what they wanted, estimated from behavior (no reformulation, no immediate return to results, time to a satisfied end) and calibrated against surveys or human ratings. Secondary: reformulation rate, clicks, time to answer. Guardrails: page speed at the 95th percentile, errors, advertiser value (so that a change does not quietly hurt the businesses who pay), and return rate over the following weeks. <em>Size and duration.</em> At search-engine traffic the sample is huge, which creates its own risk: any tiny difference will be significant. So set the smallest worthwhile effect from the decision (practical significance, C12.5) and read the interval against it. Run at least two full weeks for weekly cycles and to see novelty (a new layout may attract exploration at first). <em>Segments.</em> Query types behave differently: navigational queries (find a site), informational (get an answer), shopping. Pre-specify the cuts, since an average can hide a gain in one type and a loss in another, and treat other cuts as hypotheses (C12.7). <em>Validity.</em> Check the split before any metric; look at the effect by week and for new versus established users; check for interference (little here, since users do not affect each other's results, though advertisers do respond to changes over time). <em>Decision rule.</em> Ship if the success metric's interval is at or above the threshold and no guardrail breaches its margin. Keep a small long-term holdout to track whether the effect persists. What would change my mind: if satisfaction surveys disagreed with the behavioral success measure, I'd trust the surveys and fix the measure. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that experiment prompts at Google concern its own products at large scale <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c14-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C14-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C14 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Netflix</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You want to test a new way of presenting titles (the artwork shown for each) to members. How do you design the test, and what could mislead you?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Show half the members the new artwork and see who watches more.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Chooses member-level randomization, a primary metric, guardrails and a duration, and checks novelty.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates short-term engagement from retention, handles interactions among titles on the same page, uses new versus existing members and a long-term holdout, and states a decision rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information. The decision: adopt the new presentation if it helps members find something they enjoy. The hypothesis: better artwork increases how often members start something they finish and are happy with. <em>Unit.</em> The member, so each member sees one presentation consistently on every device. <em>Metrics.</em> A trap: a click or a play is easy to raise with striking art that misleads. So the primary should reflect satisfaction: plays that last beyond a few minutes (or completion), per member. Guardrails: members who start and quickly stop (a sign of misleading art), customer-service contacts, and cancellations. The long-run outcome is retention, which a short test cannot measure directly, so I would use a proxy (satisfying viewing in the first weeks) and say that it is a proxy (C05). <em>Interference within the page.</em> Artwork for one title competes with the others on the same row: a more striking image takes plays from neighbors. So the measure should be total satisfying viewing per member, not plays of the one title, or the gain for one title can hide a loss elsewhere (cannibalization, C08). <em>Novelty.</em> A new look draws attention at first. Plot the effect by week and compare new members (who have no old habit) with existing ones. Suppose the weekly lift is +6.0%, +3.5%, +1.8%, +1.2%, +1.0%, +1.0%. The settled effect is about +1%, and reporting the six-week average, 2.4%, or the first two weeks, 4.75%, would overstate it (Worked example 3). <em>Duration and validity.</em> At least a few weeks, whole weeks, sample-ratio check first, fixed stopping point. A 1–2% long-term holdout to check that the effect lasts and that retention is not harmed. <em>Decision rule.</em> Ship if the settled effect on satisfying viewing is above the threshold and cancellations do not rise beyond the margin. What would change my mind: if the effect is large for new members and absent for existing ones, I'd consider showing the new art first to new members. <em>Company note.</em> Netflix is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of data-informed prompts on presentation and engagement <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Netflix data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c14-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C14-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C14 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Test a redesigned device-setup flow when each user sets up a device once.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A/B test it and measure completion.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Recognizes the one-time, slow-outcome problem, picks proxies, randomizes at the device, and sets guardrails.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the proxy from the real outcome and validates the link, handles launch-period mix and the limits of changing shipped hardware, uses a holdout and qualitative evidence, and addresses privacy and accessibility.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the situation only in general terms; nothing here describes any real product. The distinguishing facts: each person sets up a device once, so there is no repeated measure per user, and the outcome that matters (still happy with the device months later) is slow. <em>The real outcome and the delay (FW-C14-08).</em> The goal is a person who is set up successfully, uses the device's key features and is satisfied weeks later. That arrives over weeks to months. So I will choose proxies that arrive early and plausibly predict it. <em>Proxies and how I'd validate them.</em> Primary: the share of new devices that complete setup within, say, 20 minutes (baseline 92%, invented). Secondary: time to complete, abandonment by step, errors, and use of one or two key features in the first week. To check that a proxy deserves trust, I'd look at past cohorts: does completing setup in 20 minutes predict still using the device at 30 days, or returning it? If not, the proxy is the wrong target (<a href=\"#gl-goodharts-law\">Goodhart's law</a>, C05). <em>Unit and randomization.</em> The device (or the person's activation), assigned when setup starts, by a hash of an identifier, 50/50, server-controlled so it can be changed. Each person is in one arm, and that is fine: the unit is the person's first setup. <em>Size.</em> Smallest worthwhile effect on completion: +1 point. With the rule of 16, 16 × 0.92 × 0.08 ÷ 0.01² = 11,776 per arm. If activations are 50,000 a day (invented), that is a day of traffic. So volume is not the problem; mix is: around a launch, early adopters differ from later buyers, and a short test sees only the first. So I would run across several weeks, and since the arms run concurrently, the mix is the same in both and the comparison is clean. <em>Guardrails.</em> Setup failures and support contacts in the first week, returns, accessibility completion (people using assistive features must not do worse), and privacy: the new flow must not collect more data than the old one or change consent steps without review. <em>Holdout and the long outcome.</em> Keep a 5% holdout on the old flow for the first four weeks, and compare the later outcomes (day-30 use, returns) when they arrive. The decision on the proxy comes first; the holdout checks whether it held. <em>Qualitative evidence.</em> Watch people set up the device (in the lab and in the field) to see where they hesitate. Numbers say whether, observation says why, and this is where the flow's design problems appear. <em>Hardware limits.</em> If the flow is part of software that can be updated from a server, the test is easy. If part of it is fixed in the device, it cannot be changed after shipping, so the test must happen before manufacture: lab studies, prototypes and early units, which are small and less randomized. I'd say which parts are which before promising a test. <em>Decision rule.</em> Ship if the completion interval is at or above +0.5 points with no guardrail breach, with the day-30 comparison reviewed when it arrives. What would change my mind: if the proxy did not predict day-30 use in past cohorts, I'd choose a different proxy or wait for the long outcome. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that experiment prompts at Apple include once-per-user flows, and <code>Public</code> that Apple describes privacy-preserving data collection <code>[V: Apple differential privacy and privacy pages; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real Apple setup flow.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c14-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C14-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C14 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you test a change to a social product's friend recommendations?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A/B test by user and measure friend requests.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Notes that users affect each other and proposes clustering or another design, and picks a meaningful primary metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the cost of clustering, picks a metric that captures lasting connections, adds harm guardrails and a spillover check, and treats safety for vulnerable users.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information. The main design problem is interference: if I randomize users, treated people send more requests and the control users receive them, so the control is not untouched (Worked example 5). <em>Unit.</em> Clusters of people who are closely connected (communities of about 100), so most of a person's friends are in the same arm. Randomize clusters 50/50. <em>Cost of clustering.</em> Observations within a cluster are correlated, so the effective sample is smaller. The design effect is about 1 + (m − 1) × ρ, where m is the cluster size and ρ the correlation within clusters. With m = 100 and ρ = 0.02 (invented), that is 1 + 99 × 0.02 = 2.98, about 3. So 1,000,000 users in 10,000 clusters give the information of about 335,000 independent users. That is still large; it matters for smaller products or smaller effects, and the intervals will be wider than a user-level test. <em>Metrics.</em> Requests sent is easy to inflate and does not show value. The primary should be lasting connections: new connections that were accepted and that the two people interacted with in the following two weeks, per user. Guardrails: reports of unwanted or harassing requests, blocks, requests ignored or declined at a high rate (a sign of poor suggestions), and effects on people who are suggested (the people being recommended are users too, and being shown to many strangers can harm them). Secondary: requests, acceptances. <em>Spillover check.</em> Compare people at the edge of treated clusters (who have some friends in the other arm) with people at the center, to see how much the arms contaminate each other. <em>Safety.</em> Suggestions can expose people: an abuser can be recommended to a victim, or a minor to adults. Review with the safety team before any test, apply stricter rules for minors, and set a stop condition on harm reports. <em>Duration and validity.</em> At least two to three weeks for the two-week interaction measure; sample-ratio check at the cluster level; novelty by time since exposure. <em>Decision rule.</em> Ship if lasting connections per user rise by a worthwhile amount with an interval above the threshold and harm guardrails within margins. What would change my mind: if clusters leak (many connections across clusters), I'd measure the leakage and say the estimate is conservative. <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prompts at Meta involve interference among connected users <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c14-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C14-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C14 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A redesigned pricing page raised sign-ups, but you suspect it hurts retention. How do you read the readout and decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Sign-ups are up, so ship,&quot; or &quot;retention is down, so don't.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Reads the guardrail and the primary together, in the same units, and considers the mix of who is signing up.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Combines the two into the quantity that matters (retained customers per visitor), notes that the guardrail was mis-specified as a rate, follows the pre-set rule while proposing a confirmation, and sets a long-term check.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd apply the order: validity first (the split check passes in this case), then guardrails, then the primary, then the mechanism. Suppose 100,000 visitors per arm (invented). Sign-ups: 4.0% in control (4,000) and 4.5% in the variant (4,500), a lift of +0.5 points. Thirty-day retention of the new sign-ups: 70% in control and 66% in the variant, a fall of 4 points with an interval of about −7 to −1. <em>Rule as written.</em> The guardrail margin on retention was −2 points; the interval's lower end (−7) breaches it. Under the pre-set rule, the verdict is not to ship as is. <em>Put the two together.</em> What matters is retained customers per visitor. Control: 4,000 × 0.70 = 2,800. Variant: 4,500 × 0.66 = 2,970. That is +170, or +6%, per 100,000 visitors, in spite of the lower retention rate. Across the interval for retention (63% to 69%): 4,500 × 0.63 = 2,835 and 4,500 × 0.69 = 3,105, which is +35 to +305, positive throughout. The explanation is a mix: the redesign brings in marginal sign-ups who retain less well, but there are more of them, and the net is positive. <em>The lesson.</em> A retention rate as a guardrail was poorly specified: a rate falls whenever the added customers are lower quality, even when the business gains. The quantity that matters is a count, retained customers per visitor, or better, the value of retained customers. But I would not reinterpret the rule after seeing the result. The disciplined course: follow the rule (hold the launch), note that the combined metric suggests a gain, and run a short confirmation test with retained customers per visitor as the primary and a guardrail on customer quality (for example, refunds and support contacts in the first month). <em>Horizon.</em> Thirty days is short: marginal sign-ups may churn later, or lower-quality customers may cost more to serve. So I'd keep a long-term holdout and compare 90-day retention and revenue. <em>Decision.</em> Hold the full launch now; run the confirmation on the better-defined metric; ship in stages if it confirms. What would change my view: if the extra sign-ups cost more to serve than they bring, the net would turn negative and the rule's concern would be right for a reason the retention rate alone did not show.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c14-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C14-08</h3>\n";
